doc.para:
switch statement 
　Using the switch statement allows you to write if ~ else if concisely. It takes the following syntax:

switch(base_expression)
{
case condition_expression1 :

case condition_expression2 :

:
:

default:

:
:
}


　You write an expression in base_expression, and this expression is evaluated first. You also write expressions in condition_expression? after case inside the block following switch.
　condition_expression? are evaluated one after another inside the block. If the evaluated result matches base_expression, execution starts from there. If it does not match condition_expression?, statements written in between are ignored. Also, if a match occurs, any subsequent case or default labels are ignored.
　default: can be omitted, but if default is written, statements following default are forcibly executed.
　To exit the block, write break;. Please be careful, as forgetting to write break and executing through to the contents of the next case is a common mistake.


	switch(a)
	{
	case 0:
		inform("a is 0");
		break;
	case 1:
		inform("b is 1");
		break;
	case 2:
		inform("b is 2");
		break;
	default:
		inform("b is not 0, 1, or 2");
	}


	switch(a)
	{
	case 0:
	case 1: // Since there is no break, case 0 passes through here
		inform("a is 0 or 1");
		break;
	case 2:
		inform("a is 2");
	} // Since there is no default, nothing is executed for values other than 0, 1, or 2


	switch(a)
	{
	case b+1: // Expressions can also be specified after case
		inform("a==b+1");
		break;
	case c+b:
		inform("a==c+b");
	}

	switch(str)
	{
	case "aiueo": // Strings can also be specified
		type=1;
		break;
	case "kakikukeko":
		type=2;
		break;
	}
